A loop over a const renders as markup even when a reactive loop elsewhere on
the page renders the same primitive.

A loop is a render slot when its body needs one -- it reads state, or holds a
component built at run time. The inliner marks a component it leaves standing
in a reactive position as a runtime instance, and that mark is on the
declaration: html's `element`, under the reactive loop below, carries it for
every node that names it. Read as "holds a runtime instance", it made every
loop of html elements a slot, and the docs site built its whole navigation in
the browser. A primitive is rendered, never instantiated, so only a component
with a body of its own counts.

deny out/none/html/index.html `for \(const item of` -- the const loop is written into the page, not rendered by a script

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import "sngl:platform/html"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

const items list[string] = ["one", "two"]

window {
    var picks list[string] = []
    html.ul {
        for var item = items {
            html.li(innerText=item)
        }
    }
    html.ul {
        for var p = picks {
            html.li(innerText=p)
        }
    }
    button(text="pick", @click { picks.push("x") })
}
-- out/none/html/index.html --
